msgid ""
"A :class:`Scene` is an :class:`~lumos.core.group.ElementGroup` of "
"**components**. Components producing a :class:`~lumos.fields.SourceList` "
"(point sources, stars, planetary systems...) are gathered on the output "
"``sources``; components producing a :class:`~lumos.fields.RadianceField` "
"(diffuse objects: disks, nebulae, backgrounds, maps...) are summed on the"
" output ``radiance``, sampled on the angular grid of the scene."
msgstr ""

#: lumos.scenes.scene:9 of
msgid "Frames"
msgstr ""

#: lumos.scenes.scene:10 of
msgid ""
"``\"celestial\"``: objects at infinity in the ICRS frame; the scene grid "
"is centred on the pointing ``(ra, dec)`` and its coordinates are angles "
"on the sky (direction cosines, small-field approximation)."
msgstr ""

#: lumos.scenes.scene:12 of
msgid ""
"``\"terrestrial\"``: a scene on the Earth seen from a site or a platform "
"at ``(latitude, longitude, altitude)``; its grid is angular, as seen from"
" the instrument."
msgstr ""

#: lumos.scenes.scene:14 of
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"``\"laboratory\"``: objects at a finite ``distance`` from the entrance "
"pupil, with coordinates in metres. Radiance is invariant along rays in "
"vacuum (Born & Wolf, *Principles of Optics*, §4.8.1), so a radiance map "
":math:`L(x, y)` at distance :math:`d` is seen as :math:`L(\\theta d)` "
"with pixels of :math:`\\Delta x / d` radians. This keeps the instrument "
"model of objects at infinity: the curvature of the waves from a close "
"object is neglected, which holds when the pupil is small compared with "
":math:`\\sqrt{\\lambda d}` (far field of the pupil); closer objects are "
"propagated explicitly as fields (lasers, lamps)."
msgstr ""

#: lumos.scenes.scene:21 of
msgid ""
"Diffuse components are evaluated on their own grids and resampled onto "
"the scene grid by area-weighted averaging, which conserves the flux "
":math:`\\sum L\\,\\Delta\\Omega` of the part inside the scene grid."
msgstr ""

#: lumos.scenes.scene.FRAMES:1 of
msgid "Frames of scenes."
msgstr ""

#: lumos.scenes.scene.Scene:1 of
msgid ""
"Group of scene components in a frame, with the outputs ``sources`` and "
"``radiance``."
msgstr ""

#: lumos.scenes.scene.Scene:3 of
msgid ""
"Components added with :meth:`add` are connected automatically according "
"to the type of their output."
msgstr ""

#: lumos.scenes.scene.Scene:5 of
msgid "Name of the scene."
msgstr ""

#: lumos.scenes.scene.Scene:6 of
msgid "``\"celestial\"`` (default), ``\"terrestrial\"`` or ``\"laboratory\"``."
msgstr ""

#: lumos.scenes.scene.Scene:7 of
msgid ""
"Fields of the :class:`SceneCombiner`: ``ra``, ``dec`` (celestial "
"pointing), ``latitude``, ``longitude``, ``altitude`` (terrestrial), "
"``distance`` (laboratory), ``pixel_scale`` and ``n_pixels`` (angular grid"
" of the scene)."
msgstr ""

#: lumos.scenes.scene.Scene.from_catalog:1 of
msgid ""
"Create the celestial scene of the star ``name`` and of its known planets "
"(REQ-CAT-6)."
msgstr ""

#: lumos.scenes.scene.Scene.from_catalog:3 of
msgid ""
"The pointing comes from SIMBAD. When the NASA Exoplanet Archive knows "
"planets around ``name``, the star takes the parameters of the archive "
"(temperature, radius, distance) and each planet becomes a "
":class:`~lumos.elements.sources.Planet`; otherwise the star comes from "
":meth:`~lumos.elements.sources.Star.from_catalog`. ``settings`` are "
"passed to the scene (grid...)."
msgstr ""

#: lumos.scenes.scene.Scene.combiner:1 of
msgid "The child holding the frame and grid of the scene."
msgstr ""

#: lumos.scenes.scene.Scene.add:1 of
msgid "Add components and connect their output to the scene (by signal type)."
msgstr ""

#: lumos.scenes.scene.Scene.add:3 of
msgid ""
"Elements with an output of type :class:`~lumos.fields.SourceList` feed "
"the ``sources`` output, those with a :class:`~lumos.fields.RadianceField`"
" output the ``radiance`` output; other elements are added without "
"connection (connect them yourself with :meth:`connect`)."
msgstr ""

#: lumos.scenes.scene.Scene.components:1 of
msgid "The components of the scene by identifier."
msgstr ""

#: lumos.scenes.scene.Scene.frame:1 of
msgid "Frame of the scene."
msgstr ""

#: lumos.scenes.scene.Scene.grid:1 of
msgid "Angular grid of the radiance of the scene."
msgstr ""

#: lumos.scenes.scene.Scene.pointing:1 of
msgid ""
"ICRS pointing of a celestial scene "
"(:class:`astropy.coordinates.SkyCoord`)."
msgstr ""

#: lumos.scenes.scene.Scene.location:1 of
msgid ""
"Site of a terrestrial scene (:class:`astropy.coordinates.EarthLocation`, "
"WGS84)."
msgstr ""

#: lumos.scenes.scene.SceneCombiner:1 of
msgid ""
"Gather the point sources and sum the radiance maps of the components of a"
" scene."
msgstr ""

#: lumos.scenes.scene.SceneCombiner:3 of
msgid ""
"It holds the frame and the grid of the scene; :class:`Scene` creates it "
"as its child ``\"combiner\"``."
msgstr ""

#: lumos.scenes.scene.SceneCombiner.transfer:1 of
msgid "Concatenate the point sources and resample and sum the radiance maps."
msgstr ""

#: lumos.scenes.scene.scene_grid:1 of
msgid ""
"Angular grid of a scene: ``n_pixels`` × ``n_pixels`` pixels of "
"``pixel_scale`` radians."
msgstr ""

#: lumos.scenes.diffuse:1 of
msgid "Parametric diffuse components of scenes (step P8.3, REQ-SCN-3)."
msgstr ""

#: lumos.scenes.diffuse:3 of
msgid ""
"Each component produces a :class:`~lumos.fields.RadianceField` (W m⁻² "
"sr⁻¹ m⁻¹) on its own angular grid of ``n_pixels`` × ``n_pixels`` pixels "
"of ``pixel_scale``, centred on its position ``(x0, y0)``, at the spectral"
" channels of the context. Pixels are averaged over ``supersample`` × "
"``supersample`` sub-pixels, which anti-aliases sharp edges. Add "
"components to a :class:`~lumos.scenes.Scene` to sum them on the scene "
"grid."
msgstr ""

#: lumos.scenes.diffuse:9 of
msgid "Compact components"
msgstr ""

#: lumos.scenes.diffuse:10 of
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
":class:`UniformDisk`, :class:`GaussianComponent`, "
":class:`SersicComponent` and :class:`InclinedRing` hold the total "
"spectral flux density ``flux_density`` :math:`F_{\\nu,0}` at "
"``reference_wavelength`` :math:`\\lambda_0`. With a shape "
":math:`S(\\theta)` normalised by its integral over the sky, the radiance "
"is"
msgstr ""

#: lumos.scenes.diffuse:14 of
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"L_\\lambda(\\theta) = "
"F_\\nu(\\lambda)\\,\\frac{c}{\\lambda^2}\\,\\frac{S(\\theta)}{\\int "
"S\\,d\\Omega},\n"
"\\qquad F_\\nu(\\lambda) = "
"F_{\\nu,0}\\,\\frac{s(\\lambda)}{s(\\lambda_0)},\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#: lumos.scenes.diffuse:17 of
msgid ""
"with the spectral shape :math:`s = 1` (``spectrum=\"flat\"``, flat in "
":math:`F_\\nu`) or the Planck function :math:`s = B_\\nu(\\lambda, T)` "
"(``spectrum=\"blackbody\"``). Flux falling outside the grid of the "
"component is lost (the normalisation uses the analytic integral of the "
"shape when it exists)."
msgstr ""

#: lumos.scenes.diffuse:21 of
msgid ""
"**Uniform disk** of radius :math:`R`: :math:`S = 1` for :math:`r \\le R`,"
" integral :math:`\\pi R^2`."
msgstr ""

#: lumos.scenes.diffuse:22 of
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"**Gaussian** of full width at half maximum :math:`w`: :math:`S = e^{-r^2 "
"/ 2\\sigma^2}` with :math:`\\sigma = w / (2\\sqrt{2\\ln 2})`, integral "
":math:`2\\pi\\sigma^2`."
msgstr ""

#: lumos.scenes.diffuse:24 of
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"**Sérsic** profile of index :math:`n` and effective radius :math:`R_e` "
"(Sérsic 1963): :math:`S = \\exp\\{-b_n[(r/R_e)^{1/n} - 1]\\}`, where "
":math:`b_n` solves :math:`\\gamma(2n, b_n) = \\Gamma(2n)/2` so that "
":math:`R_e` encloses half of the light; integral :math:`2\\pi n R_e^2 "
"e^{b_n}\\Gamma(2n) / b_n^{2n}` (Graham & Driver 2005, PASA 22, 118, eqs. "
"1-4)."
msgstr ""

#: lumos.scenes.diffuse:28 of
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"**Inclined ring** (debris disks, exo-zodiacal disks): an optically thin "
"flat ring between ``inner_radius`` and ``outer_radius`` with a surface "
"brightness :math:`\\propto r^{-p}` in its plane, seen with the "
"``inclination`` :math:`i` (0: face-on) and the major axis at "
"``position_angle``, measured from North (+y) towards East (+x). A sky "
"point :math:`(x, y)` maps to the disk-plane radius :math:`r = \\sqrt{u^2 "
"+ (v / \\cos i)^2}` with :math:`u` along the major axis and :math:`v` "
"along the minor axis; the brightness on the sky is the disk brightness "
"divided by :math:`\\cos i` (longer path through the disk), and the total "
"flux is normalised on the grid."
msgstr ""

#: lumos.scenes.diffuse:37 of
msgid "Backgrounds"
msgstr ""

#: lumos.scenes.diffuse:38 of
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
":class:`UniformBackground` and :class:`ZodiacalLight` cover their whole "
"grid with a surface brightness ``surface_brightness`` "
":math:`\\Sigma_{\\nu,0}` (e.g. Jy arcsec⁻²) at ``reference_wavelength``: "
":math:`L_\\lambda = \\Sigma_\\nu(\\lambda)\\,c/\\lambda^2`. The zodiacal "
"light has the two-component spectral shape of sunlight scattered by "
"interplanetary dust plus the thermal emission of the dust, "
":math:`s(\\lambda) = A\\,(R_\\odot / "
"1\\,\\mathrm{au})^2\\,B_\\nu(\\lambda, T_\\odot) + (1 - "
"A)\\,B_\\nu(\\lambda, T_\\mathrm{dust})`, with the albedo :math:`A` and "
"the dust temperature near 1 au (about 265 K; Leinert et al. 1998, A&AS "
"127, 1, §8): a simple model whose absolute level comes from the reference"
" brightness."
msgstr ""

#: lumos.scenes.diffuse.DiffuseComponent:1 of
msgid ""
"Base class of compact diffuse components: total flux, spectrum, position "
"and sampling."
msgstr ""

